# Auto-configures and brings up the CAN adapter (gs_usb/CANable-style, VID:PID 1d50:606f --
# see docs/wiring.md) as can0 whenever it appears: on boot, and on hot-plug. Without this it's
# left administratively DOWN after every boot/replug (SocketCAN's default), which is what
# docs/wiring.md's manual "bring the interface up before launching" step exists to fix by hand
# -- this rule makes that step unnecessary.
#
# Explicit bit timing, not `bitrate 1000000`: same 1 Mbit/s, 75% sample point (tq 63 prop-seg 5
# phase-seg1 6 phase-seg2 4 -- what the kernel's own bitrate-only calculation already picks), but
# with `sjw 4` instead of the default-computed `sjw 2`. Bench-confirmed 2026-09-15: at sjw 2 this
# adapter's CAN_RAW_ERR_FILTER traffic was ~60% error-class frames (dominated by FORM errors
# clustered at the CRC-sequence/EOF tail of the frame -- the one stretch with no bit-stuffing
# edges to resynchronize on), with `can state` flapping ACTIVE/ERROR-PASSIVE continuously. Root
# cause: the VESCs' own CAN controllers already run at their hardware max SJW (4 tq -- see
# `CAN_BTR_SJW(3)` in the bldc repo's comm/comm_can.c), while this adapter defaulted to half that
# (2 of the `sjw 1..4` its `gs_usb:` capability line advertises it can do), leaving it unable to
# track as much per-bit drift as the far end tolerates. Raising it to the adapter's own max
# dropped the error-class fraction to <0.2% and held there with zero new error-pass/error-warn
# transitions over a sustained 15s capture -- keep these five values in sync with docs/wiring.md
# if the bitrate's ever changed, and re-derive them (`ip -details link show can0` after a plain
# `bitrate <rate>` set, then bump the reported `sjw` to its max) rather than assuming they still
# apply at a different rate.
#
# No `restart-ms` here: this bench's gs_usb adapter rejects it ("Error: Device doesn't support
# restart from Bus Off", confirmed 2026-08-05) even though it does support berr-reporting (its
# CAN_RAW_ERR_FILTER traffic above wouldn't include per-frame BIT/FORM/STUFF/CRC detail
# otherwise) -- the two are evidently independent capabilities on this firmware, not one
# implying the other. A bus-off still needs a manual
# `ip link set can0 down && ip link set can0 up` (or a replug) to clear.
#
# Install (needs root -- not something this repo can do for you):
#   sudo cp tools/80-can0-up.rules /etc/udev/rules.d/
#   sudo udevadm control --reload-rules && sudo udevadm trigger
SUBSYSTEM=="net", ACTION=="add", KERNEL=="can0", \
  RUN+="/usr/sbin/ip link set $env{INTERFACE} type can tq 63 prop-seg 5 phase-seg1 6 phase-seg2 4 sjw 4", \
  RUN+="/usr/sbin/ip link set $env{INTERFACE} up"
